Hi,

I was the supervisor for John Bown's final year project.  It's fair to
say that the project suggestion was generated having read your OIC
posting on comp.arch, and the subsequent discussion.

John started with the idea of a RSSB instruction, and implemented a
one-address form (read-modify-write) of OIC.  He used 16-bit words,
and his implementation used standard TTL parts, with a couple of small
PALs for timing generation.  His timing was quite complex, as he
implemented part of the memory using register chips (fast) and the
rest using (slow) static RAMs (32K 16-bit words).  All I/O was via a
couple of UARTs, which allowed a connection to a VDU (to drive the
`front-panel' monitor) and a host computer (microVAX).  The uVAX ran a
cross-assembler, with extensive macro facilities, and an `instruction'
simulator.  I've got the board which John built in front of me; it has
about 45 chips on it (all SSI/MSI, except for memory), and uses
`wire-wrap' construction.

I'm sure that John didn't see Mike Albaugh's work; I've certainly not
seen it myself.  I would be very interested in finding out how Mike
went about the design and construction.

Trevor.
