	See below. It's more-or-less what I've posted a few times when this
subject came up.

> I'm in the process of designing  a basic OIC (what other kinds are there?)

	The ones I know of are variants of "Table Lookup", "[Reverse] subtract",
and "[Conditional] Move". 

> and I've also come to the conclusion that having magic addresses is the way
> to go, but I'm still trying to hammer out the details.

	Some machine rely more heavily on "magic addresses" than others.
The Move Machine category is pretty much _all_ magic addresses. I suppose
it's also the most practical, in some sense. At least the evidence that one
even made it into commercial use must count for something :-) I just have
a "gut" feeling that it's not "really a one-instruction computer", in some
ideological sense.

> There is also a technique which I have come
> across which removes the need for the third address in the Subtract Branch
> if Negative computer, although it requires altering the memory structure
> somewhat (you go from a linear to a 2d model). I don't know how this would
> work with your design.

	I didn't do anything so complicated. In a nutshell, the evolution
of more traditional computer architecture started with a canonical four-
address instruction:

<dst> = <src1> op <src2>, goto <next>

but folks (under pressure of extremely high memory costs, by today's
standards) noticed that long chains of instructions tended to follow
patterns, which could be exploited for "compression" by elimination of
redundancy.

	Those with random-access memory ditched <next>, as it was
overwhelmingly simply "insn+1". This change involved adding a "cache" of
sorts (the PC) for "next expected instruction" and some new instructions
to alter its value.

	Many others noticed that <dst> was often one of <src1> or <src2>.
taking this to extremes, many added a single register, the "accumulator",
which was the implicit <dst> and <src1> of most instructions. By the mid
1950's, a _very_ typical computer had

acc = acc op <src>

as its dominant instruction format, this was called a "one-address machine"

	machines with drum or delay-line memory tended toward:

acc = acc op <src>, goto <next>

this was called a "1+1 address machine", to distinguish it from another
popular form, the "Two address machine", which dispensed with acc and
used

<dst> = <dst> op <src>

later variants blended these by having some number, larger than one but
smaller than memory, of "registers" (which might actually _be_ in memory)
so <dst> might take only 2..6 bits to specify. The wildly popular IBM 360,
PDP-10, and 68000 were of this form. Modern RISCs are often "three address"
machines, but the "addresses" are limitted to the register set.

	Smaller machines often kept registers in memory, and programmers
learned to do, um, _interesting_ things by exploiting the dual nature of
these resources. For example, my machine stores the PC in location zero,
as several early minicomputers did, most notably the PDP-5, a direct
ancestor of the PDP-8, which pretty much coined the term "minicomputer".

	I pretty much followed the same sort of evolution from VanDerPoel's
machine, but chose "skip" rather than branch, to allow an implied PC,
and "borrow" rather than "minus" to facilitate unsigned arithmetic,
as I had found it difficult to reconcile the "persistence" of the
sign bit with the "limitted range" ( :-) of the skip and still do
useful work. (Note that VanDerPoel had already spec'd a "Reverse Subtract"
machine, thus including an implicit accumulator. I don't know why
H&P uses a pure memory-to-memory machine, unless possibly out of some
sense of "purity" :-)

